WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello and welcome back. This week the first thing we'll do is show you a number of case studies of effective convolutional neural networks.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.000
So why look at case studies?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Last week you learned about the basic building blocks such as convolutional layers, cooling layers and fully connected layers of convnet.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:26.000
It turns out a lot of the past few years of computer vision research has been on how to put together these basic building blocks to form effective convolutional neural networks.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
And one of the best ways for you to gain intuition yourself is to see some of these examples.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:45.000
I think just as many of you might have learned to write code by reading other people's code, I think that a good way to gain intuition of how to build confidence is to read or to see other examples of effective confidence.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:55.000
And it turns out that a neural network architecture that works well on one computer vision task often works well on other tasks as well, such as maybe on your task.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:03.000
So if someone else has trained a neural network or has figured out a neural network architecture that is very good at recognizing cats and dogs and people,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
but you have a different computer vision task that may be trying to build a self-driving car.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
You might well be able to take someone else's neural network architecture and apply that to your problem.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.000
And finally, after the next few videos, you'll be able to read some of the research papers from the field of computer vision.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:34.000
And I hope that you might find it satisfying as well. You don't have to do this as a class, but I hope you might find it satisfying to really read some of these seminal computer vision research papers and see yourself able to understand them.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
So with that, let's get started.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.000
As an outline for what we'll do in the next few videos, we'll first show you a few classic networks.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
The Lynette 5 network, which came from, I guess, the 1980s. AlexNet, which is often cited in the VGG network.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
And these are examples of pretty effective neural networks.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.000
And you see ideas from these papers that will probably be useful for your own work as well.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Then I want to show you the ResNet, or called Residual network.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.000
And you might have heard that neural networks are getting deeper and deeper.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The ResNet neural network trained a very, very deep 152-layer neural network that has some very interesting tricks, interesting ideas how to do that effectively.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
And then finally, you also see a case study of the Inception neural network.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:36.000
After seeing these neural networks, I think you have much better intuition about how to build effective convolutional neural networks.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:45.000
And even if you end up not working computer vision yourself, I think you find that a lot of the ideas from some of these examples, such as ResNet's Inception network,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.000
many of these ideas are cross-fertilizing or making their way into other disciplines.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:57.000
So even if you don't end up building computer vision applications yourself, I think you find some of these ideas very interesting and helpful for your work.
